    image: "/blog/covers/whatsapp-price-quote-templates.webp",
    content:
      'A good price quote on WhatsApp covers five things: what you are offering, a clear price, what is included, how long the price is valid, and the next step. When all five are in one message, the client can decide without a long back-and-forth. Below are 7 price quote templates for the situations small businesses run into most.\n\nReplace the parts in square brackets with your own details. Save the ones you use often as Quick Replies in the WhatsApp Business app.\n\n## 1. How do you reply when someone asks for the price?\n\nGive the price straight away instead of "please check the catalog", then ask one question to keep the chat going.\n\nExample message: "Hi [name], thanks for asking. [Product] is Rp [price] per [unit], and that includes [what is included]. How many do you need? I can work out the total with shipping for you."\n\n## 2. How do you write a detailed quote for a service?\n\nFor services like design, photography, renovation or event catering, clients need to know exactly what they get. Put each part on its own line so it is easy to read on a phone.\n\nExample message: "Hi [name], here is the quote for [service]: [task 1], [task 2] and [task 3]. Total Rp [price], [number] working days, up to [number] rounds of revisions. This price is valid until [day, date]."\n\n**Tip:** always state the revision limit and the timeline. These two cause the most misunderstandings once a project has started.\n\n## 3. How do you quote wholesale or bulk orders?\n\nShow bulk buyers the price tiers with real numbers instead of "price is negotiable".\n\nExample message: "Hi [name], prices for [product]: 1 to 11 pcs Rp [price] each, 12 to 49 pcs Rp [price], 50 pcs and up Rp [price]. The minimum wholesale order is [number] pcs. Would you like me to work out the total for the amount you have in mind?"\n\n## 4. How do you send a quote after a site visit or meeting?\n\nAfter meeting in person or visiting a site, send the quote quickly, ideally the same day. Mention what you talked about so the client remembers the context.\n\nExample message: "Hi [name], thanks for your time today. Based on what we discussed about [need], here is our quote: [short breakdown], total Rp [price]. The full quote file is attached below. I will check in with you on [day] to hear what you think."\n\n"I will check in on Thursday" is a promise. If you forget it, the client may think you are not serious. Write it down somewhere you will see it: a phone reminder, a notebook, or NANTI, which reads a WhatsApp chat you paste or screenshot and notes who, what and when.\n\n## 5. How do you answer a client who asks for a discount?\n\nDo not just cut the price. Offer options instead: a smaller package, a bigger order, or a small extra, so your price keeps its value.\n\nExample message: "I understand, [name], the budget matters. There are two options: the [smaller package] for Rp [price], or the original package with [extra] added at no cost. Which one works better for you?"\n\n## 6. How do you follow up on a quote with no reply?\n\nMost quotes go unanswered not because they were rejected, but because they got buried under other chats. Wait two or three days, then send a short reminder that is easy to answer.\n\nExample message: "Hi [name], just checking in on the quote for [product/service] I sent on [day]. Is there anything you would like me to adjust? If you need more time, no problem, just let me know roughly when."\n\n**Tip:** one or two follow-ups is enough. More than that starts to feel pushy.\n\n## 7. How do you remind a client that the price is about to expire?\n\nIf your price only holds until a certain date, for example because material costs are rising, tell the client before it passes.\n\nExample message: "Hi [name], a quick note: the quoted price of Rp [price] for [product/service] is valid until [day, date]. After that the price follows the latest material costs. If you would like to go ahead, just reply here and I will prepare the invoice."\n\n## Why do quotes so often go nowhere?\n\nQuotes rarely fail because of the price. More often nobody follows up: the seller forgets to check in, or the promise "I will send the full file tomorrow" is never kept.\n\nA few small habits help:\n\n- Use labels in WhatsApp Business such as "Quote sent" and "Waiting for reply" so open chats are easy to find.\n- Write a follow-up date for every quote in one place, such as a notebook or NANTI.\n- Check that list every morning before you answer new chats.\n\n## Key takeaways\n\n- A good quote names the product, the price, what is included, how long it is valid and the next step.\n- Answer price questions with a real number, then ask one follow-up question.\n- For services, state the revision limit and the timeline.\n- When asked for a discount, offer options instead of cutting the price.\n- Follow up on unanswered quotes after two or three days.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What should a price quote message include?\nThe product or service, the price, what is included, how long the price is valid and the next step for the client.\n\n### When is the best time to send a quote?\nAs soon as the client asks or right after a meeting, ideally the same day while the need is still fresh.\n\n### Should a quote have an expiry date?\nYes, especially if your material costs change often. An expiry date protects you and gives the client a reason to decide.\n\n### How many times can I follow up on a quote?\nOnce or twice is enough, two or three days apart. After that, let the client come back to you.',
