    image: "/blog/covers/whatsapp-customer-complaint-reply-templates.webp",
    content:
      'The best way to reply to a customer complaint on WhatsApp is in four steps: acknowledge the problem, apologize without blaming anyone, ask for proof if needed, and offer a fix with a clear time. An upset customer does not need a long message. They need to know you heard them and when it will be sorted.\n\nBelow are 7 complaint reply templates for the problems small shops and online sellers see most. Replace the parts in square brackets with your customer\'s details.\n\n## 1. How do you reply when an item arrives damaged?\n\nDo not get defensive or blame the courier straight away. Ask for a photo or unboxing video first, because you will need it for a claim with the courier or supplier.\n\nExample message: "Hi [name], I\'m really sorry your order arrived damaged. Could you send a photo of the damage and of the package? Once I have them, I\'ll get back to you today with replacement options."\n\n## 2. What if you sent the wrong item?\n\nA wrong color or size is your mistake, so own it clearly. Offer an exchange and do not make the customer pay the shipping.\n\nExample message: "Hi [name], you\'re right, that was our mistake. You ordered [correct item] and we sent [wrong item]. We\'ll ship the right one and cover the shipping. You can send the wrong item back with the return code I\'ll send you on [day, date]."\n\n## 3. What do you say when a package is late or missing?\n\nCheck the tracking before you reply, so your answer has real information instead of just "please wait". If it is stuck with the courier, say what you will do about it.\n\nExample message: "Hi [name], I checked tracking number [number]. The package has been held at the [city] hub since [date]. I\'ve contacted [courier] and I\'ll update you again tomorrow at 12 noon."\n\n**Tip:** name an exact time, not "as soon as possible". A clear time stops the customer from asking again and again.\n\n## 4. How do you reply to a customer angry about a slow response?\n\nIf you really did reply late, skip the long excuse. Apologize, then answer their original question in the same message.\n\nExample message: "Sorry for the slow reply, [name]. About your question on [topic], the answer is [short answer]. If anything is still unclear, I\'m online until [time] today."\n\n## 5. What if the customer says the item doesn\'t match the photo?\n\nThis is often about screen colors or size expectations. Listen first, ask for a comparison photo, and never open with "it looks the same in the photo".\n\nExample message: "Thanks for letting me know, [name]. Could you send a photo of the item in good light? I\'ll compare it with our stock. If it really differs from the catalog photo, you can exchange it or get a refund, and I\'ll handle it for you."\n\n## 6. How do you reply to a refund request?\n\nBe honest about the terms and the timing. Customers stay calm when they know when the money will come back, even if it takes a few days.\n\nExample message: "Sure, [name], we can process the refund. Please send your bank name, account number and account holder name. We\'ll transfer the money by [day, date] at the latest and send the transfer receipt here."\n\n## 7. What do you write when you can\'t answer yet?\n\nSometimes you need to check with the warehouse, a supplier or your team first. Not having the answer yet is fine, as long as you say when you will come back.\n\nExample message: "Hi [name], I need to check this with [warehouse/supplier] first. I\'ll get back to you today before 5 pm so you\'re not left waiting without news."\n\nThe hard part is keeping that time. With new chats coming in all day, "I\'ll get back to you at 5" is easy to lose. Write it down somewhere you will see it: a phone reminder, a notebook, or NANTI, which reads a WhatsApp chat you paste or screenshot and notes who, what and when.\n\n## Why does a clear time matter more than the apology?\n\nAn apology calms the moment, but a promised time that you actually keep is what brings the customer back. If you say "I\'ll update you tomorrow at 12" and then do it at 12, they know the problem is in good hands.\n\nA few small habits help:\n\n- Save the templates above as Quick Replies in the WhatsApp Business app.\n- Use labels like "Complaint" and "Waiting for update" so unfinished chats are easy to find.\n- Keep every promised time in one place, such as NANTI or a list on your phone, and check it each afternoon.\n\n## Key takeaways\n\n- Reply to complaints in order: acknowledge, apologize, ask for proof, give a fix and a time.\n- Ask for a photo or video before deciding on a replacement.\n- Own your mistakes clearly and do not blame the courier or the customer.\n- Always name a day and time when you promise an update.\n- Write down every "I\'ll get back to you" so you actually keep it.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### How fast should you reply to a complaint on WhatsApp?\nAim for the same day. If you don\'t have the answer yet, send a short reply first and say when you will come back.\n\n### Should you apologize if the courier caused the problem?\nYes. Apologizing for the customer\'s experience is not the same as admitting fault, and it keeps the conversation calm.\n\n### What if the customer uses rude language?\nStay polite and focus on the fix. If it goes too far, say you are ready to help once the conversation is calm again.\n\n### Can you reuse complaint templates?\nYes, as long as you always change the customer\'s name and the details so it doesn\'t feel like an automated message.',
