    slug: "free-reminder-apps-small-business",
    title: "6 Free Reminder Apps for Small Business Owners",
    excerpt:
      "Six free reminder apps for small business owners, from Google Tasks to TickTick: what reminders each offers, where it runs and one honest limitation.",
    category: "Small Business",
    date: "2026-11-11",
    readTime: "5 min",
    image: "/blog/covers/free-reminder-apps-small-business.webp",
    content:
      'Good free reminder apps for small business owners include Google Tasks, Google Keep, Microsoft To Do, Todoist, TickTick and NANTI. Each one has a free version that can alert you at a set time, and most can repeat a reminder daily, weekly or monthly. Pick the one closest to how you already work.\n\nBefore you choose, ask yourself one question: where do your work promises usually start? If they come by email, pick an app next to your inbox. If they come from WhatsApp chats with customers, suppliers and resellers, pick the fastest way to capture a chat.\n\n## 1. Is Google Tasks right if you already use Gmail and Google Calendar?\n\nGoogle Tasks works at tasks.google.com, in the side panel of Gmail and Google Calendar, and in a phone app. You can add a date and time to a task and get a notification at that time. Tasks can repeat daily, weekly, monthly or yearly, and dated tasks show up on your calendar.\n\n**Good for:** routine checks like "confirm Bu Rina\'s down payment transfer every Monday", or turning an order email into a task.\n\n**Limitation:** it is very basic, and a task that has subtasks cannot repeat.\n\n## 2. Is Google Keep right for quick notes that need a reminder?\n\nGoogle Keep is a notes app where you can add a date and time reminder to a note, including a repeating one. It runs at keep.google.com and on Android and iPhone. Keep reminders also show up in Google Tasks and Google Calendar.\n\n**Good for:** jotting down a materials shopping list, a promo idea or a tracking number, then putting a reminder on that same note.\n\n**Limitation:** because reminders live in Tasks and Calendar too, a few rules feel odd. For example, you can only edit a reminder\'s title in Calendar, Tasks or Gemini, not in Keep itself.\n\n## 3. Is Microsoft To Do right if you work in Outlook or Windows?\n\nMicrosoft To Do is free and runs on Windows, Mac, Android, iPhone and the web. You can set due dates, reminders at a date and time you choose, and tasks that repeat daily, on weekdays, weekly, monthly, yearly or on a custom pattern. The My Day view helps you plan today\'s list.\n\n**Good for:** owners who already have a Microsoft account and want a tidy task list without much setup.\n\n**Limitation:** you still type every task yourself, one by one, including promises that started in a chat.\n\n## 4. Is Todoist right if you like typing tasks quickly?\n\nTodoist has a free plan called Beginner that includes task reminders and recurring due dates. It works on the web, desktop and phones. When you give a task a date and time, Todoist adds an automatic reminder based on your settings.\n\n**Good for:** typing something like "send invoice to Pak Budi tomorrow at 10" and having it saved with the date.\n\n**Limitation:** custom reminders are a Pro feature, and snoozing a reminder only works in the mobile apps.\n\n## 5. Is TickTick right if you want a task list plus a calendar?\n\nTickTick has a free version with time reminders, repeating tasks (weekly, monthly, yearly or custom) and a calendar view. It runs on Android, iPhone, desktop and in the browser. It also has location reminders, but its official site lists them as iOS only.\n\n**Good for:** planning customer deliveries and seeing them laid out by week.\n\n**Limitation:** the free version is capped at 9 lists and 99 tasks per list, so you need to keep your lists tidy.\n\n## 6. Is NANTI right if most of your promises live in WhatsApp chats?\n\nNANTI is a web app, so you open it in your browser. You paste a copied WhatsApp chat or upload a chat screenshot, and the app picks out who, what and when. Reminders appear in the app and as browser push notifications. The free plan tracks up to 50 items.\n\n**Good for:** promises scattered across chats, like a supplier saying "the fabric ships Thursday, Kak".\n\n**Limitation:** it does not send reminders through WhatsApp, and there is no phone app to download.\n\nExample message: "Bu Sari, as we discussed, I\'ll expect the balance for the 20 t-shirts by Friday afternoon. Thank you."\n\n## How to choose in 10 minutes\n\n1. Open this week\'s work chats on WhatsApp and count the promises you almost forgot.\n2. Check which apps are already on your phone. If Google Tasks or Microsoft To Do is there, try it first.\n3. Add three real promises, such as a down payment, a supplier delivery and a reseller follow-up.\n4. Set realistic reminder times, for example one hour before each deadline.\n5. Use it for a week. If you still forget to write things down, switch to something faster to use.\n\n## Key takeaways\n\n- All six apps have a free version with time-based reminders.\n- Google Tasks and Google Keep are the easiest picks if you already use a Google account.\n- Microsoft To Do suits people who live in Outlook and Windows.\n- Todoist and TickTick do more, but some reminder features are limited on their free versions.\n- If your promises mostly start in chats, pick a tool that captures chat content quickly.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Which free reminder app is easiest for a beginner?\nGoogle Tasks and Microsoft To Do are the simplest. Type the task, set a date and time, and wait for the notification.\n\n### Can any of these apps read my WhatsApp chats automatically?\nNone of the apps on this list read your WhatsApp on their own. You still type, paste or upload a screenshot of what was said.\n\n### Are location reminders free?\nIt depends on the app. TickTick lists its location reminders as iOS only, so check the official page before you rely on one.\n\n### Should I use more than one app?\nUsually not. One place you check every morning beats three apps you rarely open.',
